          <video controls preload="metadata" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm" poster="/images/seafood-boil-shrimp-searing-blackstone.webp">
            <source src="/videos/seafood-boil-clip-1.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
          </video>
          <video controls preload="metadata" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm" poster="/images/blackstone-seafood-boil-griddle-full.webp">
            <source src="/videos/seafood-boil-clip-2.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
          </video>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Serving a Griddle Boil</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The best part of doing it this way: nothing has to be ready at the same time. Sausage and crab came off early and went straight to the table for people to start on. Shrimp came off in waves. Lobster and salmon landed last, still in their butter.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/blackstone-seafood-boil-griddle-full.webp" alt="Overhead view of the Blackstone griddle loaded with shrimp, sausage, crab, and a foil pan, with tongs plating into a tray" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Plating straight off the griddle into trays &mdash; food kept moving all night</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Nobody stood around waiting. Nobody's crab went cold while the shrimp finished. And I got to actually talk to people between batches instead of standing over a pot watching a timer.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What I'd Tell You Before You Try It</h2>

      <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">One seasoning across the whole cook.</strong> The butter carries flavor between everything on that griddle. Multiple rubs turn into mud. Pick one all-purpose rub and commit.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Shrimp goes last.</strong> It's the fastest thing on the griddle by a wide margin. Cook it first out of enthusiasm and you'll regret it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Sausage first, for the fat.</strong> Rendering it early gives everything after it something better than plain oil to cook in.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Use a foil pan for the delicate stuff.</strong> Lobster and salmon get punished by direct flat-top heat. A pan of butter on the griddle solves it without costing you space.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Char the lemons.</strong> Cut side down. It's a thirty-second decision that changes how the whole plate tastes.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Save the butter.</strong> The foil pan is the sauce. Spoon it over everything.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-mcgennis-forest text-white rounded-xl p-8 my-12">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold mb-4 tracking-tight">Worth Every Bit of the Cleanup</h3>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Stephanie's whole family around the table, Leo working the griddle next to me, Rapphie holding down the fort in his push play car, and a pile of crab legs disappearing faster than I could plate them. This is the kind of cooking that's actually about the people.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-90">
          Got a griddle boil combination we should try, or a rub you swear by for seafood? Drop it in the comments.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

